      },
      "Comments": {
        "value": "Topic: Determinant proof without expansion. Goal: Prove $\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & bc & a(b+c) \\\\ 1 & ca & b(c+a) \\\\ 1 & ab & c(a+b) \\end{vmatrix} = 0$. Method: (1) Expand third column to $ab+ac$, $bc+ba$, $ca+cb$. (2) Apply column operation $C_3 \\to C_3 + C_2$ to get common factor $(ab+bc+ca)$ in $C_3$. (3) Resulting determinant has two identical columns ($C_1$ and $C_3$ are both 1s), thus value is 0. Key property: If two columns/rows are identical, determinant is zero.",
        "order": 4
